.TH MKDIR 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
mkdir \- make directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkdir
.RB [ \-pv ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR mode ]
.IR directory ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkdir
creates each
.I directory
operand, in order.
It is the
.BR busybox (1)
applet, which has fewer options than GNU
.BR mkdir ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
A
.I directory
that cannot be created is reported on standard error, and the remaining
operands are still processed.
The exit status is then 1.
With no operand, or with an invalid option, it prints a usage message on
standard error and exits with status 1.
.PP
Without
.BR \-p ,
the parent of each
.I directory
must exist, and an existing directory or file of that name is an
error.
The operands
.B .\&
and
.B /
are the exception: they name directories that exist, and
.B mkdir
succeeds on them without doing anything, with or without
.BR \-p .
Other spellings, such as
.B ./
and
.BR .. ,
are errors without
.BR \-p .
.SS Parent directories
With
.BR \-p ,
.B mkdir
creates the missing parents of each
.IR directory ,
starting from the leftmost.
A parent or
.I directory
that exists as a directory is left unchanged and is not an error.
An existing file with the name of a parent or
.I directory
is an error.
Parents created before an error remain.
.SS Output
Nothing is written to standard output unless
.B \-v
is given.
An error is written to standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
mkdir: can\(aqt create directory \(aq\fIpath\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I reason
is the system's message, such as
.B File exists
or
.BR "No such file or directory" .
The
.I path
is the operand with any backslashes changed to slashes.
With
.BR \-p ,
it is cut after the slashes that follow the component that failed, so it
can end in a slash.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-pv ,
and may follow operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
The argument of
.B \-m
may be attached, as in
.BR \-m755 ,
or be the next argument.
A long option may be abbreviated to any unambiguous prefix, and takes
its argument after
.B =
or as the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR \fImode\fR, \fB\-\-mode=\fImode\fR
Set the mode of each
.I directory
operand that
.B mkdir
creates to
.IR mode ,
after creating it.
Parents made by
.BR \-p ,
and directories that already exist, are not changed.
.IP
The
.I mode
is an octal number from 0 to 7777, written with octal digits only, or a
symbolic mode as in
.BR chmod (1)
that modifies 0777.
A
.I mode
that begins with a digit from 0 to 7 is read as octal.
One that cannot be parsed is reported as
.B mkdir: invalid mode
.BI \(aq mode \(aq
before any directory is created, and the exit status is 1.
A symbolic clause with no
.BR u ,
.BR g ,
.BR o ,
or
.B a
before its operator is limited by the umask; see
.BR BB_UMASK .
.IP
If the mode cannot be set,
.B mkdir
reports
.B can\(aqt set permissions of directory
and the directory remains.
On Windows a valid
.I mode
has no visible effect; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-parents
Create missing parent directories, and do not treat an existing
directory as an error.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Write
.BI "created directory: \(aq" path \(aq
on standard output for each directory created, including each parent
made by
.BR \-p .
The
.I path
of a parent ends with the slashes that follow it in the operand, as in
.BR \(aqout/\(aq .
The usage message omits this option.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument only, write a usage message on standard output
and exit with status 0.
The message omits the long options and
.BR \-v .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when busybox starts, for a caller
such as
.B cmd.exe
that does not expand them.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for the programs it starts, so commands run from the kit's shell get the
shell's expansion only.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
The file mode creation mask, in octal, applied before
.B mkdir
runs.
It limits a symbolic
.B \-m
mode that has no
.BR u ,
.BR g ,
.BR o ,
or
.B a
before its operator, but since the mode has no visible effect, neither
does the mask.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Messages written to a console also depend on
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and Windows error dialogs on
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
as for every busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including
.B \-\-help
as the first argument.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or mode, no operand, or any directory that could not
be created or given its mode.
.SH EXAMPLES
Make a directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mkdir build
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make several directories:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mkdir src doc test
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make a directory and its parents, without an error if it exists:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mkdir \-p out/bin/debug
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the directories that are made:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mkdir \-pv out/lib
created directory: \(aqout/\(aq
created directory: \(aqout/lib\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make a directory and its parents on another drive:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mkdir \-p d:/work/tmp
.EE
.RE
.PP
Take a lock, or print
.B busy
if another script holds it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mkdir lock.d 2>/dev/null || echo busy
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR mkdir :
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-m
only checks its mode; see below.
.IP \(bu 2
.B mkdir .\&
and
.B mkdir /
succeed even without
.BR \-p .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-v
is accepted although the usage message omits it.
.IP \(bu 2
Options not listed above, such as
.BR \-Z ,
.BR \-\-context ,
and
.BR \-\-version ,
are invalid.
.PP
Windows has no Unix mode.
A new directory gets the permissions it inherits from its parent
directory.
.B \-m
can only set the read-only attribute, and never does for a directory,
whatever the
.IR mode ,
so it only checks that the
.I mode
is valid; see
.BR chmod (1).
.PP
Paths may have drive letters and backslashes.
Backslashes in an operand are changed to slashes, so messages and
.B \-v
output show slashes.
A path may be absolute, as in
.IR c:/dir ,
relative, or relative to a drive, as in
.IR c:dir .
With
.BR \-p ,
the drive or UNC share that begins a path, as in
.I q:/dir
or
.IR //host/share/dir ,
is one component that must exist.
If it does not, the error names it, as in
.BR \(aqq:/\(aq .
.PP
Names are not case sensitive on most volumes, so
.B mkdir Foo
fails with
.B File exists
when
.I foo
exists.
Names may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later; see
.BR busybox (1).
A name that Windows does not allow, such as one containing
.BR ? ,
fails with
.BR "Invalid argument" .
Avoid Windows device names:
.B mkdir con
fails with
.BR "Invalid argument" ,
but
.B mkdir nul
reports success and can make a directory that ordinary paths cannot
reach.
The full path of a directory, counting the current directory when the
operand is relative, must be shorter than 248 characters.
A longer path fails with
.BR "No such file or directory" .
.PP
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B mkdir
expands wildcards in its arguments itself; see
.BR BB_GLOBBING .
A pattern that matches nothing is used as it is, and fails with
.BR "Invalid argument" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR chmod (1),
.BR install (1),
.BR rmdir (1)
